author_final_remarks.value:
We sincerely thank all reviewers, AC, SAC, and PC for their valuable efforts and constructive feedback. 
We appreciate the reviewers’ recognition of our work’s contributions, including **the well-scoped and meaningful benchmark** (Reviewers Qfha, ucZB, and Cw6n), **comprehensive evaluation** (Reviewers ucZB, 7WdM, and Cw6n), **the novel LLM-free evaluation framework** (Reviewers Qfha, 7WdM, and Cw6n), **the effectiveness of FAVOR-Train** (Reviewer 7WdM), and **the well-written paper** (Reviewers Qfha and Cw6n).

---
During the rebuttal and discussion phases, **we provided point-by-point responses addressing most of the reviewers’ concerns**.

Key resolutions include:

(1) **task interdependency analysis** revealing difficulty hierarchies and cross-task correlations;

(2) **rigorous validation through human performance baselines** demonstrating reasonable task design and substantial room for model improvement;

(3) **analysis of FAVOR-Train’s effectiveness** showing distributional separation (KL divergence ≈0.32 bits) between FAVOR-Train and FAVOR-Bench, along with consistent performance gains on external benchmarks (TVBench and MotionBench);

(4) **validation of the LLM-free metric's reliability** via strong correlations with GPT-assisted evaluation (Spearman’s ρ=0.77, *p*<0.0001; Pearson’s r=0.86, *p*<0.0001).

---
Following the reviewers’ suggestions, **we will enhance the revised version** by:

(1) adding explicit analysis of task-difficulty hierarchies and deeper discussions on failure modes;

(2) highlighting the reliability analysis of the LLM-free framework more prominently;

(3) including a dedicated ethics section that extends existing copyright considerations and addresses dataset bias and broader societal impacts;

(4) incorporating human performance baselines and related analysis to better contextualize MLLM performance.

---
We believe FAVOR-Bench makes critical contributions to the community: **it provides a comprehensive benchmark for fine-grained video motion understanding**, featuring 1,776 videos from diverse perspectives, 8,184 carefully curated QA pairs, and both GPT-assisted and LLM-free open-ended evaluation. Together with FAVOR-Train’s 17,152 annotated videos, our work offers practical resources for developing stronger video understanding models capable of comprehending detailed temporal dynamics.

We again thank all reviewers, AC, SAC, and PC, for the time and effort devoted to reviewing our work and helping us improve this paper.

